This version converts the number to text and visits each character. A possible minus sign is ignored, while every other character is converted from `'0'` to its numeric value.

The useful edge cases are `0` and negative numbers. The string for `0` still contains one digit, and the minus sign in a negative number does not affect the result.

```java
class Solution {
    public int largestDigit(int n) {
        long value = n;
        if (value < 0) {
            value = -value;
        }

        String digits = Long.toString(value);
        int largest = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < digits.length(); i++) {
            int digit = digits.charAt(i) - '0';
            if (digit > largest) {
                largest = digit;
            }
        }

        return largest;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(d)` for scanning the digits.
Space complexity: `O(d)` because the number is stored as a string.

I kept the arithmetic version small. Take the last digit with `% 10`, compare it with the answer, then remove that digit with `/ 10`. Using `long long` inside the method also makes taking the absolute value safe for the smallest `int` value.

For example, with `n = 5082`, the digits checked are `2`, `8`, `0`, and `5`, so the result becomes `8`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int largestDigit(int n) {
        long long value = n;
        if (value < 0) {
            value = -value;
        }

        int answer = 0;
        if (value == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        while (value > 0) {
            answer = std::max(answer, static_cast<int>(value % 10));
            value /= 10;
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(d)`, where `d` is the number of digits.
Space complexity: `O(1)`, not counting the local variables.

A set gives a direct way to keep only the different digits seen in the number. After collecting them, `last()` from a `TreeSet` gives the largest one. This uses more setup than just keeping one variable, but the code makes the ordering automatic.

If the input is `-500`, the minus sign is not inserted. The set contains only `0` and `5`, so the returned value is `5`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int largestDigit(int n) {
        long value = n;
        if (value < 0) {
            value = -value;
        }

        java.util.TreeSet<Integer> digits = new java.util.TreeSet<>();
        if (value == 0) {
            digits.add(0);
        }

        while (value > 0) {
            digits.add((int) (value % 10));
            value /= 10;
        }

        return digits.last();
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(d log 10)`, which is effectively `O(d)` since there are only ten possible digits.
Space complexity: `O(1)` because the set can hold at most ten values.

Since a decimal digit can only be from `0` to `9`, a small frequency list is enough. The code counts each digit first, then checks the list from `9` down to `0`. The first present digit is the answer.

Dry run for `n = 4071`: the counts for `4`, `0`, `7`, and `1` are marked. Checking downward finds `7` first, so that is returned.

```python
class Solution:
    def largestDigit(self, n):
        value = abs(n)
        present = [False] * 10

        if value == 0:
            present[0] = True
        else:
            while value > 0:
                present[value % 10] = True
                value //= 10

        for digit in range(9, -1, -1):
            if present[digit]:
                return digit

        return 0
```

The running time is `O(d + 10)`, which is `O(d)` in practice. Space usage is `O(1)` because the list always has ten entries.

Here the number is reduced one digit at a time through recursion. The last digit is compared with the result for the remaining prefix. The helper receives a non-negative `long long`, so negative input and the smallest integer value are handled before recursion starts.

For `n = 926`, the calls eventually check `6`, then `2`, then `9`, and the largest value returned is `9`.

```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    int findLargest(long long value) {
        if (value < 10) {
            return static_cast<int>(value);
        }

        int lastDigit = static_cast<int>(value % 10);
        int largestFromPrefix = findLargest(value / 10);
        return std::max(lastDigit, largestFromPrefix);
    }

public:
    int largestDigit(int n) {
        long long value = n;
        if (value < 0) {
            value = -value;
        }
        return findLargest(value);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(d)` because there is one recursive call per digit.
Space complexity: `O(d)` for the recursion stack.

## Intuition
It requires the comparison among each digit of the given number to find the largest so we use digit extraction approach. 

## Approach
1. Intialize a variable which stores and track the largest digit till the time with value negative infinity as a number could be positive or  negative or 0 also .
2. Cover the edge case: if n=0 then return the number that is 0. Because then only 0 is there so the largest digit will be the number itself.
3. Start a while loop to get the lasst digit until the given number becomes 0.
4. perform digit extraction logic:
        4.1. intialize a variable (say r) which stores and tracks the last digit of the given number on each time when loop runs.
        4.2. Now store the largest number between the current largest value and remainder r using Math.max()
        4.3. Remove rhe last digit of the given number to perform the same operation on rest of rtrhe digits by dividing that number by 10 and stores back the quotient into variable n.
5. End while loop
6. return the largest variable which contains final and largest digit of the number n.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public int largestDigit(int n) {
        int largest =Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        if(n==0) return n;
        while(n>0){
            int r = n % 10;
            largest = Math.max(largest, r);
            n /= 10;
        }
        return largest;
    }
}
```

